==Controversies==
=== Hong Kong Journalists Association ===
In June 2024 ''Wall Street Journal'' editors learned that ''WSJ'' journalist [[Selina Cheng]] was a candidate for the leadership  of the [[Hong Kong Journalists Association]], a local press union. Cheng's editor demanded that she withdraw from the election and from participation in the union. However, the right to stand for election and participate in a union without employer consent are established under Hong Kong employment law. Cheng did not withdraw and was elected to the leadership role.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.economist.com/china/2024/07/24/the-noose-around-the-press-in-hong-kong-tightens | title=The noose around the press in Hong Kong tightens | newspaper=The Economist }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.barrons.com/news/ex-wsj-reporter-says-fired-over-role-in-hong-kong-press-union-002d09da | title=Ex-WSJ Reporter Says Fired over Role in Hong Kong Press Union }}</ref><ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/article/2024/jul/17/wall-street-journal-fires-new-chair-of-hong-kong-journalists-association | title=Wall Street Journal fires new chair of Hong Kong Journalists Association | newspaper=The Guardian | date=July 17, 2024 | last1=Hawkins | first1=Amy }}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://hongkongfp.com/2024/07/17/breaking-chair-of-hong-kong-journalists-association-selina-cheng-fired-by-wall-street-journal-weeks-after-election/ | title=Wall Street Journal fires Hong Kong reporter Selina Cheng | date=July 17, 2024 }}</ref> In July 2024, the paper's chief editor Gordon Fairclough travelled to Hong Kong and fired her from her role at the ''WSJ''. In response to press queries, the ''WSJ'' declined to comment on Cheng's case except to acknowledge there had been "restructuring". Cheng responded that the restructuring was a layoff of one person.<ref name="Cheng2024"/>

The paper's action against Cheng attracted criticism from media organisations, press unions and human rights proponents across the globe.<ref name="Walden 2024">{{cite web | last1=Walden | first1=Max | last2=Cai | first2=Jenny | title=Wall Street Journal accused of double standards after sacking Hong Kong journalist | date=July 19, 2024 | url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2024-07-19/wall-street-journal-sacks-selina-cheng-press-freedom-concerns/104117976 | work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|access-date=September 12, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.scmp.com/news/hong-kong/politics/article/3271052/overseas-groups-step-support-hong-kongs-selina-cheng-over-wall-street-journal-sacking | title=Global press groups support Hong Kong reporter over Wall Street Journal sacking | first=Connor | last=Mycroft | date=July 19, 2024 | access-date=September 12, 2024}}</ref> Chinese state media, meanwhile, celebrated her sacking, with the ''[[Global Times]]'', a tabloid owned by the [[Chinese Communist Party]], calling the press union "a malignant tumour that harms the city's safety and security".<ref name="Walden 2024"/><ref>{{cite web | url=https://cpj.org/2024/08/cpj-calls-for-support-for-hong-kong-journalists-amid-growing-pressure-trial-delays/ | title=CPJ calls for support for Hong Kong journalists amid growing pressure, trial delays | date=August 12, 2024 }}</ref> Asked by the ''[[South China Morning Post]]'' about his views about the reason for the dismissal, Hong Kong's security minister [[Chris Tang]] mentioned what he saw as the possibility that the journal might have wanted not to be associated with the press union, the latter being in his view "not a well-recognized association".<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 26, 2024 |title=The Wall Street Journal told staff to 'avoid press freedom advocacy in Hong Kong' |url=https://www.scmp.com/news/hong-kong/politics/article/3272069/wall-street-journal-firing-over-press-freedom-stance-hong-kong-selina-cheng-says |access-date=July 31, 2024 |work=[[South China Morning Post]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241108011002/https://www.scmp.com/news/hong-kong/politics/article/3272069/wall-street-journal-firing-over-press-freedom-stance-hong-kong-selina-cheng-says |archive-date=November 8, 2024 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> Cheng said that the ''Wall Street Journal'' had fired her to avoid being seen as advocating press freedom in the city.<ref name="Cheng2024">{{Cite web |last=Cheng |first=Selina |title=I pushed for press freedom in Hong Kong. The Wall Street Journal fired me. |url=https://www.cjr.org/first_person/selina-cheng-wall-street-journal-gershkovich-evan-china-hong-kong-apple.php |access-date=July 31, 2024 |website=Columbia Journalism Review |language=en}}</ref> In July 2024, the [[NewsGuild-CWA]] and Independent Association of Publishers' Employees, the union for [[Dow Jones & Company|Dow Jones]], expressed their support for Cheng.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.mingpao.com/ins/%e6%b8%af%e8%81%9e/article/20240724/s00001/1721787451949 |title=IAPE發起聯署籲《華爾街日報》讓鄭嘉如復職 指美加早禁解僱支持工會者 |trans-title=IAPE Initiates Joint Petition Calling on ''The Wall Street Journal'' to Reinstate Selina Cheng; Pointing Out That the United States and Canada Had Long Banned The Firing of Union Supporters. |date=July 24, 2024 |website=[[Ming Pao]] |access-date=December 28, 2025 |language=zh}}</ref> The latter launched a petition calling on ''WSJ'' to reinstate Cheng, which has garnered 335 employee signatures in support.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.rfi.fr/tw/%E4%B8%AD%E5%9C%8B/20240828-%E9%80%BE%E4%B8%89%E7%99%BE%E4%BA%BA%E9%9F%BF%E6%87%89%E7%BE%8E%E5%9C%8B%E5%B7%A5%E6%9C%83%E8%81%AF%E7%BD%B2-%E4%BF%83-%E8%8F%AF%E6%97%A5-%E8%AE%93%E8%A8%98%E5%8D%94%E4%B8%BB%E5%B8%AD%E9%84%AD%E5%98%89%E5%A6%82%E5%BE%A9%E8%81%B7 |title=逾三百人響應美國工會聯署 促《華日》讓記協主席鄭嘉如復職 |trans-title=Over 300 Respond to U.S. Union Petition, Urging ''The Wall Street Journal'' to Reinstate Journalist Association Chairperson Selina Cheng. |date=August 28, 2024 |website=[[Radio France Internationale]] |last=Mak |first=Yin-ting |access-date=December 28, 2025 |language=zh}}</ref> In November 2024, Cheng filed a civil lawsuit in Hong Kong against the newspaper over her firing.<ref>{{Cite news |date=November 12, 2024 |title=Hong Kong journalist to sue Wall Street Journal over sacking |url=https://www.rfa.org/english/china/2024/11/12/china-hong-kong-wsj-journalist-sues-dismissal/ |access-date=November 13, 2024 |work=[[Radio Free Asia]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Leung |first=Kanis |date=November 12, 2024 |title=Hong Kong reporter says she'll sue Wall Street Journal for dismissal because of her union role |url=https://apnews.com/article/selina-cheng-wsj-dismissal-hong-kong-65c74d2fd85b90bf63678238522cd8c3 |access-date=November 13, 2024 |website=[[Associated Press]] |language=en}}</ref>

In February 2025, criminal proceedings against the ''WSJ'' began in Hong Kong.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Chow |first1=Fiona |title=Hong Kong press union head sues former employer Wall Street Journal over firing |url=https://www.scmp.com/news/hong-kong/law-and-crime/article/3298495/hong-kong-press-union-head-launches-private-prosecution-against-ex-employer |website=[[South China Morning Post|SCMP]] |access-date=February 14, 2025 |date=February 13, 2025}}</ref>

[[Centrism in Hong Kong|Hong Kong centrist]] lawmaker [[Tik Chi-yuen]] criticized the ''Wall Street Journal'' for [[self-censorship]] and fear of offending the authorities, which seriously impacts press freedom in Hong Kong.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.hk01.com/政情/1039196 |title=記協主席鄭嘉如被《華爾街日報》辭退 狄志遠：衝擊香港核心價值 |trans-title=Journalists Association Chairperson Selina Cheng Dismissed by ''The Wall Street Journal''; Tik Chi-yuen: This Impacts Hong Kong's Core Values. |date=July 17, 2024 |website=[[HK01]] |last=Cheng |first=Bo-sheng |access-date=December 28, 2025 |language=zh}}</ref> [[Ben Lam (politician)|Ben Lam]], a former [[Mong Kok East (constituency)|Mong Kok East]] politician residing in the UK, described ''WSJ''{{'s}} actions as supporting authoritarianism and compared their chief editor to [[Walter Duranty]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://thechasernews.co.uk/%E4%BE%86%E8%AB%96%EF%BD%9C%E6%B7%AA%E7%82%BA%E6%A5%B5%E6%AC%8A%E5%B9%AB%E5%85%87%E7%9A%84%E5%A4%96%E5%9C%8B%E8%A8%98%E8%80%85/ |title=來論｜淪為極權幫兇的外國記者 |trans-title=To Comment｜Foreign Journalists Reduced to Aiding Authoritarianism. |date=July 18, 2024 |website=[[Photon Media|Chaser News]] |last=Lam |first=Ben |author-link=Ben Lam (politician) |access-date=December 28, 2025 |language=zh}}</ref>

In a series of articles about News Corp, Australian online news outlet [[Crikey]] described Cheng's firing as part of "turmoil" at the ''WSJ'' and that "the global purging of newsrooms is as much about creating a politically correct workforce of reliable journalistic cadres as it is about simply saving costs."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Warren |first=Christopher |date=July 30, 2024 |title=Watch Hong Kong (not Reno) to understand what News Corp is really up to |url=https://www.crikey.com.au/2024/07/31/news-corp-lachlan-james-hong-kong/ |access-date=July 31, 2024 |website=Crikey |language=en-US}}</ref> The [[United States-China Economic and Security Review Commission|U.S.-China Economic and Security Review Commission]] annual report noted the significance of the event as demonstrating "the pressures on foreign media to self-censor in line with the [[CCP]]'s requirements". It concluded that the ''WSJ''{{'}}s action "calls into question claims that foreign businesses have been unaffected by the new atmosphere following the passage of the [[2020 Hong Kong national security law|NSL]] and [[Safeguarding National Security Ordinance|Article 23 Ordinance]]".<ref>{{cite book |title=2024 REPORT TO CONGRESS |date=Nov 2024 |publisher=U.S.-CHINA ECONOMIC AND SECURITY REVIEW COMMISSION |page=698 |url=https://www.uscc.gov/sites/default/files/2024-11/2024_Annual_Report_to_Congress.pdf |chapter=10}}</ref>

=== President Trump feud ===
On July 17, 2025, the newspaper reported that, in 2003, Donald Trump had sent convicted child sex offender [[Jeffrey Epstein]] a [[Jeffrey Epstein's birthday book|50th birthday card]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Palazzolo |first=Khadeeja Safdar and Joe |title=Exclusive {{!}} Jeffrey Epstein's Friends Sent Him Bawdy Letters for a 50th Birthday Album. One Was From Donald Trump. |url=https://www.wsj.com/politics/trump-jeffrey-epstein-birthday-letter-we-have-certain-things-in-common-f918d796 |access-date=August 2, 2025 |website=WSJ |date=July 17, 2025 |language=en-US}}</ref> The next day, Trump (then a sitting president) sued the newspaper, alleging two counts of defamation and seeking at least $10 billion for each count. It was believed to be the first time a sitting president had ever sued a journalist or media outlet for personal defamation.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Williams |first1=Michael |last2=Berman |first2=Dan |last3=Stelter |first3=Brian |date=July 18, 2025 |title=Trump files libel lawsuit over Wall Street Journal report on Jeffrey Epstein's birthday letters |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/07/18/politics/trump-wall-street-journal-jeffrey-epstein |access-date=July 20, 2025 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref> The case was assigned to Judge [[Darrin P. Gayles|Darrin Gayles]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Woodward |first=Alex |date=July 21, 2025 |title=Obama-appointed Florida judge to preside over Trump's $10B suit against Murdoch and WSJ |url=https://www.inkl.com/news/obama-appointed-florida-judge-to-preside-over-trump-s-10b-suit-against-murdoch-and-wsj |access-date=July 21, 2025 |website=inkl |language=en}}</ref> On July 21, the ''Journal'' was also removed from the White House press pool for Trump's trip to Scotland.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Reilly |first=Brian Stelter, Liam |date=July 21, 2025 |title=Trump White House removes Wall Street Journal from Scotland trip press pool over Epstein report {{!}} CNN Business |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/07/21/media/trump-bans-wsj-wall-street-journal-scotland-press-pool-epstein |access-date=August 2, 2025 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref> Two days later, the ''Journal'' reported that Trump was informed by the Justice Department in May that his name was found in the [[Epstein Files]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Leary |first=Sadie Gurman, Annie Linskey, Josh Dawsey and Alex |title=Exclusive {{!}} Justice Department Told Trump in May That His Name Is Among Many in the Epstein Files |url=https://www.wsj.com/politics/justice-department-told-trump-name-in-epstein-files-727a8038 |access-date=August 2, 2025 |website=WSJ |date=July 23, 2025 |language=en-US}}</ref>

